P1 = "Economic sanctions are often presented as a middle path between diplomacy and war. They appear to allow a government to act firmly without risking its soldiers. Their record, however, is mixed. Studies suggest that sanctions succeed most often when goals are modest, when the target depends heavily on the sanctioning state, and when many countries act together. Broad sanctions may also harm ordinary citizens far more than the leaders they are meant to influence, since rulers can protect themselves and blame foreigners for the hardship. Partly for this reason, governments have turned to targeted measures, such as freezing the assets of particular officials. These reduce civilian suffering but are not free from difficulty, since wealthy elites can hide money through third countries. Sanctions, then, are not useless, but they work best as one tool among several, not as a substitute for a clear political strategy."

P2 = "For much of the last century, a small number of editors decided which events reached the public. Their power was often criticised, yet it also provided a filter: a rumour usually had to pass at least one checker before it was printed. Social media has removed much of that gate. Anyone can now publish, and this has given a voice to people whom traditional newspapers ignored. The same openness, however, rewards speed and emotion, because posts that provoke anger tend to spread further than careful corrections. The result is not simply that people are misled; many begin to doubt every source, true or false. Some propose stricter platform rules, while others fear such rules would simply hand the editors' old power to technology companies. No easy answer exists, but readers can at least ask who produced a claim and what evidence supports it."

def p(text, q):
    return {"p": text, "q": q}

